  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 PM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nEarly morning showers and isolated thunderstorms across central and \nsouth central Kansas moved off to the east fairly quickly resulting \nin sunny sky. Remnant low level moisture will remain across much of \nwestern and central Kansas despite the passage of the shortwave \ntrough, due to absence of low level cold/dry advection from the \nnorth. This will likely set the stage for development of fog, \nespecially up north. 12Z HREF probs show 40-60% chance of 1/4 mile \nvisibility or less generally along/northeast of a Ness City to \nKinsley to Pratt line, so we will add in some areas of fog early \nSunday morning across these areas. Any early morning fog should \nscatter out to a sunny sky by mid to late morning. This will likely \nresult in a delayed warm-up, but even these areas up northeast \nshould still top out in the upper 70s to near 80 for late afternoon \nhighs. Highest temperatures in the mid to upper 80s likely along the \nOklahoma line in the Red Hills region.\n\nA major, potentially long-duration rainfall event is expected to \nunfold from New Mexico northeast across West Texas into southwestern \nKansas mid to late week (particularly late Tuesday through \nThursday), as deep Pacific moisture from remnant tropical system \nPolo interacts with low level gulf moisture and a polar trough axis \ndigging down into the Southwest. All major global models and their \nassociated ensemble systems are starting to line up in a large \nscale, potential excessive rainfall event which would include much \nof southwest Kansas and the surrounding region. Modeled precipitable \nwater (PW) fields are exceedingly high for a large area for a \nprolonged period of time, which is quite unusual (i.e. PW of 1.60\"\nfor a 48-hr period Late Monday evening through late Wednesday \nevening with highest prob 1.75\"+ at DDC of 23% Tuesday Night). \n\nThe mid to late week meteorology will be very interesting to see \nunfold as the deep tropical cyclone remnants become infused into the \nslow-moving polar trough, effectively forming a large extra-tropical \ncyclone which would likely move very slowly east across the \nSouthern/Central Plains upon development. This new low would have \nunrestricted gulf moisture infusion as well, and the end result \ncould be very high 3-4 day rainfall totals well in excess of two \ninches over a fairly large area. The latest 13Z NBM run this morning \nhas a 90th percentile 48-hr QPF of around 4 inches over much of \nKansas, Oklahoma and North Texas/Texas Panhandle. For this reason, \nthe WPC Excessive Rainfall Outlook will carry a SLIGHT Risk across \nall of our DDC CWA Tuesday and Wednesday with a MODERATE Risk \nincluding our far southwest Kansas counties Tuesday. The Wednesday \nexcessive rainfall outlook may be upgraded as well as confidence \nincreases in high rainfall amounts over that 24-hour period.",
